# sveltekit-fsd

Use this skill for the FSD surface only: which directory a new thing goes in, and
how it is imported. Once a slice exists, its content — markup, business rules,
request shapes — is the engineer's work.

Create slices with the CLI rather than by hand, so the route file, the `index.ts`
public API, the `.svelte.ts` naming for runes and the segment layout stay
consistent. Hand-written slices are exactly what the two linters then report.

This file is emitted at `.agents/skills/sveltekit-fsd/SKILL.md` in the repository
root, symlinked from `.claude/skills/sveltekit-fsd`. Agents that load neither
should follow `AGENTS.md`, which points at [docs/fsd.md]({{docsFromSkill}}) — the
fuller convention, and the source of truth if this file and it ever disagree.

**FSD methodology questions are not this skill's job.** Whether a thing should be
an entity at all, when to extract from a page, cross-import and `@x` patterns,
how strict to be — that is the `feature-sliced-design` skill, which `init` wrote
next to this one. This one only covers driving the generator in this repo.

## When to use this skill

- A new page, screen or route is requested, even without the word "page".
- A new reusable feature or business object is requested, even without the words
  "feature" or "entity".
- Several routes need shared chrome (a sidebar, an auth card) → a layout.
- The project needs API error handling or auth for the first time.
- An existing slice needs a segment it does not have yet (state, requests,
  helpers, an error catalog).

Examples that all use this skill:

- "add a settings screen" → `generate page settings`
- "the dashboard should require login" → `generate page dashboard --auth`
- "admin pages need a shared sidebar" → `generate layout admin`
- "loans need to be reusable across screens" → `generate slice entities loan`
- "show a real message when saving fails" → `add error-handling`, then `--errors`
  on the slice that owns the failing call

## When not to use it

- Writing the actual markup, state, or request body inside a generated file
- Styling, Tailwind theme edits, or shadcn-svelte component customisation
- `load` functions, form actions, hooks — those are SvelteKit's files, not FSD's
- Bug fixes, refactors, or dependency work
- Deciding whether a layer *should* exist — that is the FSD skill
- Any repository without sveltekit-fsd.config.json at its root

## Before running the CLI

1. Read `sveltekit-fsd.config.json`. `features.errorHandling` and `features.auth`
   decide which flags are even legal, `routesDir` says where route files go, and
   `locale` says which language generated copy is written in.
2. Look for the slice first. `{{srcDir}}/pages/`, `{{srcDir}}/features/`,
   `{{srcDir}}/entities/` — a name that already exists means you are extending,
   not creating, and the flags differ.
3. Check `{{routesDir}}/` for an existing route before choosing `--route`. A page
   routed from a group is not where the default would look.
4. Confirm the work is on a branch you may commit to (`git status -sb`) and
   preserve unrelated changes.

## Commands

Run from the project root. **Pass every required value explicitly** — an
omitted value opens a prompt, and in a non-interactive shell that exits 1
without writing anything.

~~~bash
sveltekit-fsd wizard
sveltekit-fsd init [--locale th|en] [--no-install] [--no-hooks]
sveltekit-fsd generate page <name...> [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--auth] [--api] [--errors] [-r <root>] [--defaults]
sveltekit-fsd generate slice <features|entities|widgets> <name...> [-s <segments...>] [-r <root>] [--errors] [--defaults]
sveltekit-fsd generate layout <name> [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--guard] [--defaults]
sveltekit-fsd add error-handling [-y] [--no-install]
sveltekit-fsd add auth [-y] [--no-install]
sveltekit-fsd add prettier [-y] [--no-install]
sveltekit-fsd config show
~~~

`--defaults` answers every remaining question, so scripted and agent use needs
`--defaults` (or the full set of flags) on every generate command.

Feature gates, which fail loudly rather than generating something broken:

- `--auth` and `--guard` require `features.auth` → run `add auth` first
- `--errors`, `--api` (or legacy `--model`) and the `api` segment require `features.errorHandling` →
  run `add error-handling` first
- `add auth` installs error handling itself if it is missing; do not run both
- `add prettier` formats the whole project once, on purpose — it puts
  `prettier --check .` on the lint script, and adding the check without the pass
  leaves `{{lintCommand}}` failing on every file. Commit that pass by itself.
  Once it is installed, generated files come out formatted; do not reformat them
  by hand

## Extending, not rewriting

**Re-run the same command to add to something that exists.** Do not hand-write
the file, and do not delete the slice to regenerate it.

~~~bash
sveltekit-fsd generate slice features checkout --segments ui           # ui/ only
sveltekit-fsd generate slice features checkout --segments ui,model     # adds model/
sveltekit-fsd generate page dashboard --errors --defaults              # adds the catalog
~~~

Only the missing files are written; new exports are appended to the slice's
`index.ts`; every existing file is left exactly as it is. If there is nothing to
add, the command says so and writes nothing — that is the expected answer, not a
failure to work around.

Two consequences worth knowing:

- The page component is an existing file, so a guard or API hooks added later are
  not wired to anything yet. The command prints the line to add; add it.
- A page already routed from elsewhere does not get a second route file.

## Contracts the generated code relies on

Breaking one of these is silent — the build passes and the app is wrong.

- **A module that uses runes is named `*.svelte.ts`, and imported without the
  `.ts`.** `$state` in a plain `.ts` is not a compile error, it is an undefined
  function at runtime. `import { useThing } from "./model/thing.svelte"`.
- **`createQuery` / `createMutation` / `useQueryClient` are called during
  component initialisation.** They read Svelte context; calling one from an event
  handler throws, and the message does not explain why.
- **Their options are a function, not an object** — `createQuery(() => ({...}))`.
  That is what keeps a key built from `$state` reactive. An object literal
  freezes the options at first render.
- **Page-specific requests live in `pages/<name>/api/`.** Use `--api`; the old
  `--model` flag is retained as a compatibility alias. `model/` is for frontend
  state, schemas, and business logic.
- **CLI ergonomics follow the FSD CLI.** `generate page|slice` accepts multiple
  names, names may contain a slice group such as `employee/employee-record`,
  `-s` accepts comma- or space-separated segments, and `-r` selects another FSD
  root inside `{{srcDir}}`. Keep `{{srcDir}}/lib/` for SvelteKit's `$lib`.
- **Move reused requests to their owner.** Entity-related requests belong in
  `entities/<name>/api/`, reused user actions in `features/<name>/api/`, and
  generic client or CRUD primitives in `shared/api/`.
- **A route file renders a page and nothing else.** The title lives in the page
  component's own `<svelte:head>`; there is nothing to re-export.
- **Imports point downwards, and a slice is entered through its `index.ts`.**
  `{{alias}}/features/x`, never `{{alias}}/features/x/ui/x.svelte`. Inside a
  slice the import is relative. `$lib/` is SvelteKit's own `{{srcDir}}/lib/`,
  not a layer: shared code goes in `{{srcDir}}/shared/`, imported as
  `{{alias}}/shared/...`.
- **Never render `error.message` as UI copy.** Add the code to the domain's own
  catalog (`model/<name>-errors.ts`) and let the fallback name the action that
  failed. A backend message is written for a log.
- **One catalog per domain, never one global map.** `generate ... --errors`
  creates it in the right place.
- **A failure the user must fix stays inline, one they only need to know goes to
  a toast.** `<FormError>` is the inline half; a toast helper takes a finished
  sentence, and resolving a code stays with the domain that raises it.
- **A second consumer means moving code to its owner.** Reusable domain requests
  belong in `entities/<name>/api/`, reused user actions in
  `features/<name>/api/`, and generic client or CRUD primitives in
  `shared/api/`. If the owner is unclear, keep the code in the consuming page
  until the boundary is stable. Read docs/fsd.md before creating a layer.{{#if (eq locale "th")}}
- **Thai copy: no negative tracking, nothing Thai inside `font-mono`, and line
  heights loosened per text size.** Tone marks stack above and below, and a mono
  face loaded with the latin subset has no Thai glyph to render.{{/if}}
- **A catalog is a plain object, so i18n needs no second shape.** In a project
  with a translator, build it where it is rendered —
  `catalogs={[{ CONFLICT: t("conflict") }]}` — and keep the codes as the keys;
  they are the API's contract, not copy. The CLI's `locale` only decides what the
  first draft reads like.
- **The access token stays in the module variable** in
  `{{srcDir}}/shared/auth/access-token.ts`. Never move it to `localStorage`, and
  never remove the single-flight guard in `shared/api/client.ts` — a backend that
  rotates refresh tokens logs the user out mid-session without it.
- **The QueryClient is created inside the provider component, not at module
  level.** On the server that module is evaluated once per process; a shared
  client hands one user's cached data to the next.
- **`{{routesDir}}/` renders pages, not features.** ESLint enforces this one.

## Verification

After generating and filling in a slice:

~~~bash
{{lintCommand}}      # eslint (per-file boundary) + steiger (whole-tree)
{{packageManager}} run check      # svelte-check — this is the typecheck
{{packageManager}} run build
~~~

`svelte-check`, not `tsc`: a `.svelte` file's types only exist once the compiler
has looked at it, and `svelte-kit sync` has to have run for `{{alias}}/` to
resolve at all — which `check` does for you.

Both linters report a wrong-way or slice-internal import — ESLint at the file as
you type it, steiger over the whole tree. steiger also reports what no single
file can show: `fsd/insignificant-slice` on a brand-new slice means nothing
imports it yet — either wire it up or inline it back into its single consumer; it
is the linter working, not a false positive.

Where the CLI wrote a `client.test.ts`, run the project's test command too. Do not
delete that test to make a change pass: the single-flight 401 refresh and the
no-refresh-on-`/auth/*` rule are the two things in this codebase that fail
silently in a browser.

Report the exact output, and say plainly which generated TODOs are still TODOs.
